~ start
Prime Minister Zawadzki: Mr President, in Warsaw we do not do small talk before the map. The map is the small talk.
- Offer a French brigade on rotation => brigade
- Offer a joint arms purchase => arms
- Ask how Poland sees the Union's future => future
~ brigade
Prime Minister Zawadzki: A brigade. For the first time in a hundred years France would stand where we stand. I will see that it is photographed.
- Make it permanent => permanent
- Make it a one-year rotation => rotation
~ permanent
Prime Minister Zawadzki: Permanent. My opposition will call it a gift and my generals a miracle.
=> END
~ rotation
Prime Minister Zawadzki: A rotation is respectable. The troops rotate, the gratitude should not.
=> END
~ arms
Prime Minister Zawadzki: A joint purchase. Our factories and yours could build in tandem, and our generals could swap their catalogues for once.
- Choose French artillery => artillery
- Choose a shared design => shared
~ artillery
Prime Minister Zawadzki: French artillery, a respectable choice, and my Americans will frown into their coffee.
=> END
~ shared
Prime Minister Zawadzki: A shared design takes five years. We have, I should say, two.
=> END
~ future
Prime Minister Zawadzki: Poland wants a union that defends, that trades, and that does not lecture us on the sequence.
- Say France wants a stronger union => stronger
- Say France wants a looser union => looser
~ stronger
Prime Minister Zawadzki: Stronger, then. We shall see whether you mean it when the bill arrives.
=> END
~ looser
Prime Minister Zawadzki: A looser union is easy to promise. Whether it would hold in winter is another matter.
=> END
